{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785236509,"nonce":"+1mYc3CFssam4UGHo5ZRcehJ6lw=","message":"**A second thing now needs this tier: the Export's overlay rung has also never executed.**\n\nADR-0062 s4 landed `export.rs`, whose `ExportRung::Overlay` declares `mount(2)`/`umount2(2)` inline (the `farm::reflink` precedent — no new dependency), behind a capability check and unreachable off Linux. Its unit tests cover the pure parts — the mount **option string** (`redirect_dir=on` and `nfs_export=on` are both asserted present, each proven by a mutation that drops one) and the rung-availability decision — but the `mount(2)` call itself has run nowhere.\n\nSo this ticket's blocker is no longer about one test file. The missing tier — a privileged Linux host with a Rust toolchain — now gates:\n\n1. `crates/scarab-server/tests/changeset_overlay.rs`, the twelve marker assertions (the original scope);\n2. `export.rs`'s overlay rung actually mounting, and the resulting upper layer being readable by `read_change_set`;\n3. therefore the **only** configuration in which ADR-0062's change set is *exact* rather than the stat-cache approximation.\n\nPoint 3 is the part that changes this ticket's priority. Every test that currently passes exercises the **copy** rung, whose change set is the `(size, mtime, ctime)` approximation. The exact path — the one the ADR's whole part 3 argument rests on, and the reason it claims the rungs \"do not differ in correctness\" — is the path with no executed test at all.\n\nSuggested addition to the acceptance criteria:\n\n- [ ] The overlay Export rung mounts, a write through it lands in `upper/`, and `read_change_set` on that upper returns the exact change set — end to end, in the same recipe\n\nAlso worth stating in the recipe's output: which kernel and filesystem the run covered, and **which rung each assertion exercised**. ADR-0062 already requires a build to report its rung; a test tier that does not report the same thing can report a pass for the cheap path and be read as covering the expensive one.","files":null}]}